WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.702
-Our next guest is an
Emmy Award-winning

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:04.204
writer, comedian,
and actress you know from

00:00:04.204 --> 00:00:06.739
her critically acclaimed series,
"Abbott Elementary."

00:00:06.739 --> 00:00:10.042
The second season premieres
this Wednesday, September 21st,

00:00:10.042 --> 00:00:11.110
on ABC.

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:12.278
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.278 --> 00:00:14.213
-And no more bringing
my problems into school.

00:00:14.213 --> 00:00:16.415
That was old
right-sided-part Janine.

00:00:16.415 --> 00:00:17.817
I'm a fully formed adult,

00:00:17.817 --> 00:00:21.987
and this year
is gonna be...different.

00:00:21.987 --> 00:00:24.857
What the...
What in the world?

00:00:24.857 --> 00:00:28.094
-Eagles! Go, Eagles!

00:00:28.094 --> 00:00:29.295
[ Indistinct conversations ]

00:00:29.295 --> 00:00:30.997
-Ava.

00:00:30.997 --> 00:00:34.267
Why is a man cooking ribs
in my parking spot?

00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:35.334
What is happening?

00:00:35.334 --> 00:00:36.802
-It's game day, baby.

00:00:36.802 --> 00:00:38.371
-It's Development Week.
-Here's a development.

00:00:38.371 --> 00:00:40.106
You either got to park
on the street

00:00:40.106 --> 00:00:41.607
or give me $50 for your spot.

00:00:41.607 --> 00:00:44.277
I'm making extra money...
for the school.

00:00:44.277 --> 00:00:45.879
-Please welcome back to the show

00:00:45.879 --> 00:00:48.181
our friend Quinta Brunson,
everybody!

00:00:48.181 --> 00:00:50.183
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:01:03.162 --> 00:01:04.731
Hello, Quinta.
-Hi, Seth.

00:01:04.731 --> 00:01:07.200
-You look fantastic.
-Thank you.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:10.170
[ Cheers and applause continue ]
Hi, everybody.

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:12.205
Are they normally that nice?

00:01:12.205 --> 00:01:14.073
-They're very --
No, they're only this nice

00:01:14.073 --> 00:01:15.208
when people deserve it.

00:01:15.208 --> 00:01:17.309
-Oh, my goodness.
-And congratulations.

00:01:17.309 --> 00:01:19.579
Not only did you win an Emmy
for writing,

00:01:19.579 --> 00:01:21.447
the show won
three Emmys overall.

00:01:21.447 --> 00:01:23.415
Was it a -- was it a fun night?

00:01:23.415 --> 00:01:26.418
[ Cheers and applause ]
-It was so fun.

00:01:26.418 --> 00:01:27.787
You were there.
-I was.

00:01:27.787 --> 00:01:29.521
-Part of the fun of the night
was getting to see you.

00:01:29.521 --> 00:01:30.890
Always a treat.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:33.959
But, you know, that was my
first time at the Emmys, period.

00:01:33.959 --> 00:01:37.597
So to go as a nominee
and then have the opportunity

00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:40.867
to not only win,
but have a castmate win

00:01:40.867 --> 00:01:42.935
and the casting department --
just incredible.

00:01:42.935 --> 00:01:45.104
-And your castmate --
I was lucky enough

00:01:45.104 --> 00:01:48.641
to be onstage with Amy Poehler
when Sheryl Lee Ralph won.

00:01:48.641 --> 00:01:53.613
And she not only
came onstage and gave

00:01:53.613 --> 00:01:55.414
one of the greatest speeches
I've ever seen,

00:01:55.414 --> 00:01:58.384
but she did seem
genuinely shocked in the moment.

00:01:58.384 --> 00:02:00.386
You were at her table.
-I was at her table.

00:02:00.386 --> 00:02:01.688
She was totally shocked.

00:02:01.688 --> 00:02:03.456
Let me just say first,
you and Amy Poehler

00:02:03.456 --> 00:02:04.456
could not have been better.

00:02:04.456 --> 00:02:05.725
One of my favorite people.

00:02:05.725 --> 00:02:07.727
Amy doesn't know it,
but she is one of my favorite --

00:02:07.727 --> 00:02:09.262
She's one of my comedy moms.

00:02:09.262 --> 00:02:11.063
And I was, like, "Oh, my God.
Wow, look at that.

00:02:11.063 --> 00:02:13.032
It would be amazing if,
you know,

00:02:13.032 --> 00:02:14.200
they said somebody from
'Abbot Elementary.'

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:15.434
Hmm."

00:02:15.434 --> 00:02:20.640
And then I just talked to
Sheryl the week before,

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.908
and we had this beautiful talk
about how this is

00:02:22.908 --> 00:02:26.179
a first-season show on
network TV with 13 episodes.

00:02:26.179 --> 00:02:29.014
It's amazing to just be
nominated and be in the room.

00:02:29.014 --> 00:02:31.484
And we had this beautiful talk
with each other of, like,

00:02:31.484 --> 00:02:33.653
it didn't matter if we won.

00:02:33.653 --> 00:02:36.622
And we didn't think that
we would win anything.

00:02:36.622 --> 00:02:38.023
Especially Sheryl, who's like,

00:02:38.023 --> 00:02:41.828
"You know, I'm just really happy
to be here and be nominated.

00:02:41.828 --> 00:02:42.995
And you know what?

00:02:42.995 --> 00:02:44.130
Hopefully,
the show will be around

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:45.632
and there's
plenty of time to win."

00:02:45.632 --> 00:02:49.402
So she was really
in this place of just

00:02:49.402 --> 00:02:51.504
pure joy with being nominated.

00:02:51.504 --> 00:02:55.207
So when you guys called her
name, I looked at her,

00:02:55.207 --> 00:02:58.411
and she -- I knew --
I know Sheryl very well.

00:02:58.411 --> 00:03:00.980
Sheryl is very dramatic,
as you guys could see.

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:02.682
I know that,
in her brain, she said,

00:03:02.682 --> 00:03:03.883
"This is a dream.

00:03:03.883 --> 00:03:06.351
This is not real."
"I'm not going to get up."

00:03:06.351 --> 00:03:08.354
And her whole body
went into shock.

00:03:08.354 --> 00:03:09.388
And, you know,
everyone was like,

00:03:09.388 --> 00:03:10.689
"Sheryl, they said your name."

00:03:10.689 --> 00:03:12.391
But I know she was like,
"This is 'The Wizard of Oz.'"

00:03:12.391 --> 00:03:13.593
Like, this -- I'm in a scene of
'The Wizard of Oz.'

00:03:13.593 --> 00:03:14.894
Better wake up."

00:03:14.894 --> 00:03:19.031
So I had to physically get up
and go shake her to say,

00:03:19.031 --> 00:03:20.999
"This is real!"
and get her up.

00:03:20.999 --> 00:03:23.168
And her husband's like,
"You won, you won, you won."

00:03:23.168 --> 00:03:24.437
We're like, "Right there.

00:03:24.437 --> 00:03:26.172
They said -- That's you, you,
Sheryl Lee Ralph."

00:03:26.172 --> 00:03:28.140
She's like, "Me?"
"Sheryl Lee Ralph, you."

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:30.743
So, we get her up, and my
co-star Tyler James Williams --

00:03:30.743 --> 00:03:32.812
I whispered -- I was like,
"You have to walk her

00:03:32.812 --> 00:03:34.646
on the stage, because there's
a chance she might

00:03:34.646 --> 00:03:37.416
turn back
thinking this is fake."

00:03:37.416 --> 00:03:39.285
So Tyler walked her
on the stage.

00:03:39.285 --> 00:03:41.353
And I think it was
once she was up there

00:03:41.353 --> 00:03:44.757
that she really realized
that she had won.

00:03:44.757 --> 00:03:47.560
And Sheryl comes into
the set singing.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:49.028
Like, she comes in going...

00:03:49.028 --> 00:03:50.462
♪ Good morning ♪

00:03:50.462 --> 00:03:51.797
...every single morning.

00:03:51.797 --> 00:03:53.699
So I think the first thing she
could do, out of joy,

00:03:53.699 --> 00:03:55.101
was just sing,
and it was so beautiful.

00:03:55.101 --> 00:03:58.438
-Well, it was so beautiful
and surprising,

00:03:58.438 --> 00:04:00.072
because the first half
of the moment

00:04:00.072 --> 00:04:02.074
was everything you've described,
someone who was in shock.

00:04:02.074 --> 00:04:03.909
And, usually,
the second half of that

00:04:03.909 --> 00:04:06.879
is someone who can't find
the words for the moment.

00:04:06.879 --> 00:04:10.716
And, yet, she delivered a speech
that was as polished

00:04:10.716 --> 00:04:12.918
and as finished
as anything you've ever seen.

00:04:12.918 --> 00:04:14.320
-That's Sheryl.
-It felt like an one-act play.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:15.388
It was beautiful.

00:04:15.388 --> 00:04:17.323
-That is all day with
Sheryl Lee Ralph.

00:04:17.323 --> 00:04:18.825
-Yeah.
-That is how she is.

00:04:18.825 --> 00:04:21.994
So to me -- I could just cry now
because it was beautiful

00:04:21.994 --> 00:04:24.830
seeing her be the most herself
in that moment, too.

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:27.566
There's something very pure
about her being

00:04:27.566 --> 00:04:30.136
exactly who she is
while accepting that award

00:04:30.136 --> 00:04:31.604
and making -- I'm getting teary.

00:04:31.604 --> 00:04:34.140
But making a beautiful -- a
beautiful night for everybody.

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:36.942
-It was also --
It seems crazy to say this now,

00:04:36.942 --> 00:04:40.512
but a network comedy getting
nominated is a throwback to,

00:04:40.512 --> 00:04:42.648
you know,
a previous era of the Emmys.

00:04:42.648 --> 00:04:44.383
We're not used to seeing that.
-Right.

00:04:44.383 --> 00:04:47.453
-You wanted this to be
a network television show

00:04:47.453 --> 00:04:49.588
at a time when
I feel a lot of creators

00:04:49.588 --> 00:04:51.357
maybe are going
the other way.

00:04:51.357 --> 00:04:54.126
-Yes. You know,
we've talked about this before,

00:04:54.126 --> 00:04:55.461
but I love network television.

00:04:55.461 --> 00:04:57.896
I love the accessibility of it.

00:04:57.896 --> 00:04:59.599
People laugh at me
when I say this,

00:04:59.599 --> 00:05:02.168
but it is something
people can watch in prison,

00:05:02.168 --> 00:05:03.469
which is a huge demographic.

00:05:03.469 --> 00:05:05.338
But it's not about that.
It's about accessibility.

00:05:05.338 --> 00:05:06.839
-Yeah.

00:05:06.839 --> 00:05:09.374
-It is about accessibility
and people being able to

00:05:09.374 --> 00:05:11.076
watch network TV from anywhere.

00:05:11.076 --> 00:05:14.346
And I feel like
my generation, Millennials,

00:05:14.346 --> 00:05:18.384
started veering toward
the cable and streaming space,

00:05:18.384 --> 00:05:19.986
and for one, I think they were

00:05:19.986 --> 00:05:21.453
pushed away from network
for a while.

00:05:21.453 --> 00:05:23.289
There was
just a misunderstanding

00:05:23.289 --> 00:05:25.124
between the
previous generation and ours.

00:05:25.124 --> 00:05:29.929
But after so many creators went
over and proved kind of, like,

00:05:29.929 --> 00:05:32.598
the value of our stories
in network and streaming,

00:05:32.598 --> 00:05:35.635
it feels like now
network kind of gets it,

00:05:35.635 --> 00:05:37.836
and, thus, something like
"Abbott Elementary"

00:05:37.836 --> 00:05:39.271
is able to be born.

00:05:39.271 --> 00:05:44.143
And I'm happy
that it's on network TV.

00:05:44.143 --> 00:05:46.879
I love that families
watch it together.

00:05:46.879 --> 00:05:49.549
-Yeah, I mean, that's how my
family watched television,

00:05:49.549 --> 00:05:51.317
obviously, growing up,
was that way.

00:05:51.317 --> 00:05:53.719
And another thing
that happened is,

00:05:53.719 --> 00:05:55.788
you had a successful
network television show,

00:05:55.788 --> 00:05:57.056
and, again,
it's another throwback.

00:05:57.056 --> 00:05:58.858
You did 13 your first season.

00:05:58.858 --> 00:06:01.661
You're doing
a 22-episode season,

00:06:01.661 --> 00:06:04.730
which now --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:06.999
I mean, that was the norm,

00:06:06.999 --> 00:06:09.568
but that is an exception
to the rule now for comedy.

00:06:09.568 --> 00:06:10.770
How has it been so far?

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:12.204
Obviously,
you're in the middle of it.

00:06:12.204 --> 00:06:13.372
-I'm in the middle of it.

00:06:13.372 --> 00:06:18.511
So, you know,
the producer in me is like,

00:06:18.511 --> 00:06:21.413
"Why are you on
'Seth Meyers' right now?"

00:06:21.413 --> 00:06:24.183
But then, also,
the producer in me is like,

00:06:24.183 --> 00:06:25.250
"That's good marketing.

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:26.285
We need it for the show.

00:06:26.285 --> 00:06:27.653
That's good publicity."

00:06:27.653 --> 00:06:29.422
But then I'm like,
"Man, I really wish my actress

00:06:29.422 --> 00:06:31.824
was on set so we didn't have to
rearrange her schedule

00:06:31.824 --> 00:06:33.925
to do --"
So it's -- All of my brains

00:06:33.925 --> 00:06:37.597
are having a fight at the same
time with 22 episodes.

00:06:37.597 --> 00:06:40.432
But I will say,
I have such a supportive system

00:06:40.432 --> 00:06:45.071
in my co-producers and
my writers and my crew and cast.

00:06:45.071 --> 00:06:47.607
So, I think we are
doing a good job at it,

00:06:47.607 --> 00:06:49.474
but 22 is crazy.

00:06:49.474 --> 00:06:50.910
I don't know how you do
this every night, you know?

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:53.646
-Yeah, but we don't have to
write scripts

00:06:53.646 --> 00:06:56.115
that connect to the next day
and characters.

00:06:56.115 --> 00:06:59.251
I mean, and we -- Because we
can't get ahead of it,

00:06:59.251 --> 00:07:01.053
because it's about --
You know, so --

00:07:01.053 --> 00:07:02.221
Still, thank you, though,
for saying that.

00:07:02.221 --> 00:07:04.056
-Do you do any drugs?
I've been trying --

00:07:04.056 --> 00:07:05.791
-Do I do any drugs?
Thank you for asking.

00:07:05.791 --> 00:07:07.260
No one asks.

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:08.393
-I want to know what
everyone's secret is.

00:07:08.393 --> 00:07:10.496
I'm like, "Does anyone have
different time?"

00:07:10.496 --> 00:07:11.630
-Hey, my -- The last thing
I want to ask.

00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:13.999
I know your mom is a teacher,

00:07:13.999 --> 00:07:17.069
and that must make the show
extra special for her.

00:07:17.069 --> 00:07:19.004
Did -- She's on the East Coast.

00:07:19.004 --> 00:07:20.672
Did you get to talk to her
the night of the Emmys?

00:07:20.672 --> 00:07:23.442
-I did. I called her when
I could, you know,

00:07:23.442 --> 00:07:26.111
come up for air, and it was
about 12:00 her time.

00:07:26.111 --> 00:07:28.014
And she was super-proud,

00:07:28.014 --> 00:07:30.582
but what was sweeter
was how proud my dad was.

00:07:30.582 --> 00:07:32.250
My dad doesn't watch
awards shows.

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:35.420
He doesn't care about anything.
He watches, like --

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:38.357
You know, he'll watch paint dry
before he watches

00:07:38.357 --> 00:07:40.092
an awards show
and will genuinely find it

00:07:40.092 --> 00:07:42.228
more interesting.

00:07:42.228 --> 00:07:44.930
But he actually --
My sister said

00:07:44.930 --> 00:07:47.967
he was watching the Oscars
like -- like it was the --

00:07:47.967 --> 00:07:49.969
I'm sorry, the Emmys
like it was the Super Bowl.

00:07:49.969 --> 00:07:51.737
Like, he watched the carpet.

00:07:51.737 --> 00:07:54.306
He watched everything
leading up to it.

00:07:54.306 --> 00:07:57.943
And hearing that
he was really proud of me

00:07:57.943 --> 00:08:00.713
was perhaps the highlight
of my night.

00:08:00.713 --> 00:08:03.916
So it was really sweet.
-Well, you all deserve it.

00:08:03.916 --> 00:08:05.317
-Thank you.
-Thanks for being back.

00:08:05.317 --> 00:08:08.254
I just love having you here.
You guys, that's Quinta Brunson.

00:08:08.254 --> 00:08:10.522
Don't miss the
second-season premiere of

00:08:10.522 --> 00:08:13.793
"Abbot Elementary" and then the
21 episodes after that.

00:08:13.793 --> 00:08:15.594
It's Wednesdays
at 9:00 p.m. on ABC.

00:08:15.594 --> 00:08:18.030
We'll be right back with music
from Courtney Barnett.

